WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:07.699
Hey Wisecrack, Jared again.

00:00:07.699 --> 00:00:11.319
And this week we’re delving into the dark and scandalous world of politics

00:00:11.320 --> 00:00:13.260
with a show that goes right for the jugular:

00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:14.260
House of Cards.

00:00:14.260 --> 00:00:15.100
The American version.

00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:17.880
Starting as a Congressional Whip and
the head of a non-profit

00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:21.540
the show chronicles power couple  Frank and Claire Underwood’s quest for power

00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:23.940
and the presidency at any cost.

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:26.560
One could easily link the popularity
of House of Cards

00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:30.260
to our increasing cynicism about 
the current state of American politics.

00:00:30.260 --> 00:00:33.840
But many American political dramas are either uncritical of our system

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:39.080
or offers us a morally righteous character who triumphs against the adversity of a broken government.

00:00:39.080 --> 00:00:41.520
President Jed: "We must do better
and we will do better."

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:43.580
In the end, we're always offered redemption.

00:00:43.580 --> 00:00:45.300
If the system is temporarily broken

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:48.600
it at least pertains a mechanism for
good to triumph over evil.

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:55.320
House of Cards rejects this narrative and instead offers us a process where raw power and cunning wins every time.

00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:58.820
Despite this, the show isn’t a tale of
evil overcoming good.

00:00:58.820 --> 00:01:01.480
Rather, the question that drives House of Cards 
seems to be:

00:01:01.480 --> 00:01:04.020
Is there more to politics than pure spectacle?

00:01:04.020 --> 00:01:08.580
So without further ado, welcome to this Wisecrack Edition on the philosophy of House of Cards.

00:01:08.580 --> 00:01:11.320
Part one: Aesthetics, Theater and Politics

00:01:11.320 --> 00:01:17.540
In the cynical world of House of Cards,characters are always maintaining an image, both public and private.

00:01:17.540 --> 00:01:20.520
For the careful viewer, it becomes abundantly
apparent that the show’s creators

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:24.640
are obsessed with aesthetics, that is art and beauty within the show.

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:27.760
Important dealings are often done
while reflecting on art.

00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:29.860
Claire’s appearance is carefully manicured

00:01:29.860 --> 00:01:33.100
with her wardrobe not only
the subject of frequent dialogue

00:01:33.100 --> 00:01:35.100
but real-life fashion listicles.

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:38.220
But once you peel away the pretty aesthetics
and art references

00:01:38.220 --> 00:01:40.780
what lies behind the world of House of Cards is...

00:01:40.780 --> 00:01:41.840
Well...

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:42.780
nothing....

00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:43.560
We’ll get to that.

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:45.760
House of Cards preoccupation with aesthetics

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:48.960
also manifests itself in the show's
frequent tribute to theater

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:51.760
and, more specifically, William Shakespeare.

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:56.600
Frank often uses soliloquies and asides, a theatrical device used heavily by Shakespeare

00:01:56.600 --> 00:01:59.660
wherein a character’s inner
thoughts are explicated to the audience.

00:01:59.660 --> 00:02:02.400
Others have noted that much of the show’s
plot and characters

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:06.260
are reminiscent of Shakespeare classics like Macbeth and Richard III,

00:02:06.260 --> 00:02:09.780
which inspire Frank’s charming asides
about his terrible behavior.

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:11.100
Frank: "When you're fresh meat..."

00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:11.780
"KILL"

00:02:11.780 --> 00:02:13.580
"And throw them something fresher."

00:02:13.580 --> 00:02:15.420
Petrov, in his visit to the White House

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:18.980
even quotes Gorbachev quoting Richard III’s 
famous opening line.

00:02:18.980 --> 00:02:21.540
Petrov: "...of an end to our winter of discontent."

00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:25.240
Theater is just one facet of the political
philosophy in House of Cards.

00:02:25.240 --> 00:02:29.200
Theater, aesthetics, and art, all point to the 
more fundamental concept

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:31.360
that appearance drives politics.

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:35.500
Politics is theater, or, at the very least, its own kind of performance.

00:02:35.500 --> 00:02:38.600
For instance, Frank visits his father
to seem more human

00:02:38.600 --> 00:02:40.440
Frank: "Oh, I wouldn't be here if I had a choice."

00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:42.200
"But I have to do these sorta things now."

00:02:42.200 --> 00:02:43.780
"Makes me seem more human."

00:02:43.780 --> 00:02:47.380
before revealing his true intentions.

00:02:47.380 --> 00:02:49.700
His breaking of the fourth wall to address the viewer

00:02:49.700 --> 00:02:52.540
is more than just a convenient way
to develop his character

00:02:52.540 --> 00:02:57.560
it’s an important way to blur the distinction between the show and reality and theater in politics.

00:02:57.560 --> 00:03:01.800
Contemporary commentators bemoan the fact that politics has become entertainment

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:05.460
with catchphrases and slogans replacing 
real political argument.

00:03:05.460 --> 00:03:07.020
*crowd cheering*
Frank: "A vote for Frank Underwood"

00:03:07.020 --> 00:03:09.180
"is a vote for America Works!"

00:03:09.180 --> 00:03:10.400
We see it in the show, too.

00:03:10.460 --> 00:03:13.960
Frank destroys Michael Kern’s career
over an op-ed he didn’t even write.

00:03:13.960 --> 00:03:15.300
And Kern self-destructs

00:03:15.300 --> 00:03:18.060
when he’s unable to keep pace
with media sensationalism.

00:03:18.060 --> 00:03:21.980
A brick through Frank’s window serves as a symbol against out of control teachers,

00:03:21.980 --> 00:03:24.460
which, of course, Frank fabricated himself.

00:03:24.460 --> 00:03:28.720
To clarify, those commentators are totally right about
the out-of-control theater of politics

00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:30.680
but, let’s complicate this for a minute.

00:03:30.680 --> 00:03:35.100
According to German philosopher Hannah Arendt the realm of appearances is the realm of politics.

00:03:35.100 --> 00:03:40.800
The idea of stripping away appearances to get at the core of things is pointless because they’re the same.

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:45.680
This idea originates in the ancient Greek Polis, where politics meant entering into the Agora

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:48.880
where individuals could speak and be heard
in front of their fellow Greeks.

00:03:48.880 --> 00:03:53.140
In this public realm, it was only the words,
rhetoric and deeds of citizens

00:03:53.140 --> 00:03:54.640
that were to be judged by their peers.

00:03:54.640 --> 00:03:59.880
In other words, one’s political life was identical to how they appeared to others, kind of like an actor.

00:03:59.880 --> 00:04:03.380
This idea is deeply rooted in how
we experience the world.

00:04:03.380 --> 00:04:10.760
For humans, Arendt says, “appearance--something that can be seen or heard by others as well as ourselves, constitutes reality.”

00:04:10.760 --> 00:04:13.540
Frank Underwood understands this concept 
when he says:

00:04:13.540 --> 00:04:16.780
Frank: "We are nothing more or less than
what we choose to reveal."

00:04:16.780 --> 00:04:19.240
"What I am to Claire is not what I am to Zoe"

00:04:19.240 --> 00:04:22.280
"just as Zoe is not to me, what she is to her father."

00:04:22.280 --> 00:04:23.780
Another way to read this, of course

00:04:23.780 --> 00:04:25.600
is that Frank is a sociopath.

00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:30.140
And what he chooses to reveal to us is a carefully crafted manipulation to get us to like him.

00:04:30.140 --> 00:04:33.380
Seriously, how is it that we still don’t hate
Frank Underwood?

00:04:33.380 --> 00:04:38.540
The easiest criticism to leverage against Frank is his lack of sincerity and frequent use of deception.

00:04:38.540 --> 00:04:43.560
President Walker: "Peter Russo is dead."

00:04:43.560 --> 00:04:44.680
"Suicide."

00:04:44.680 --> 00:04:48.740
The idea that appearances are deceptive and unreliable is really really old.

00:04:48.740 --> 00:04:52.660
One of the first things philosophy students
learn is Plato’s allegory of the cave,

00:04:52.660 --> 00:04:56.140
where we have to disillusion ourselves of the misleading
shadows on the wall

00:04:56.140 --> 00:04:58.200
and discover the “true”world behind them.

00:04:58.200 --> 00:05:03.220
Karl Marx used this Platonic distinction between appearance and reality in his "Theory of Ideology".

00:05:03.220 --> 00:05:07.400
Even the Matrix is just a modern rehashing of this idea dating back to the Greeks.

00:05:07.400 --> 00:05:10.960
Those news commenters complaining that 
modern political media has become a

00:05:10.960 --> 00:05:14.940
grotesque spectacle concealing what
really goes on behind closed doors

00:05:14.940 --> 00:05:17.980
are the modern torch-bearers
of this millennia-old argument.

00:05:17.980 --> 00:05:21.900
Frank and Arendt undermine one of the central tenants of this tradition —

00:05:21.900 --> 00:05:25.180
that there lies an authentic reality 
behind the world of appearance.

00:05:25.180 --> 00:05:28.220
For Arendt and Frank, appearance is reality.

00:05:28.220 --> 00:05:32.220
If we can’t hate Frank Underwood, it’s because there is no stable identity to hate.

00:05:32.220 --> 00:05:34.460
He’s a sometimes good husband to Claire

00:05:34.460 --> 00:05:36.200
a manipulative murderer to Zoe

00:05:36.200 --> 00:05:38.720
a kind boss and one-time lover to Meechum

00:05:38.720 --> 00:05:40.420
a supportive friend to Freddy

00:05:40.420 --> 00:05:42.080
a job creator for the masses

00:05:42.080 --> 00:05:43.660
and a two-timing liar to...

00:05:43.660 --> 00:05:45.000
well everybody else.

00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:48.460
Another angle of this problem is seen in
Remy and Jackie’s relationship,

00:05:48.460 --> 00:05:52.640
who are ultimately unable to reconcile 
their professional lives with their authentic selves.

00:05:52.640 --> 00:05:56.080
Through the show’s intermingling of art, theater, and aesthetics,

00:05:56.080 --> 00:05:59.420
it challenges old ideas about how reality operates.

00:05:59.420 --> 00:06:02.620
The idea behind this argument isn’t that
Frank Underwood is good

00:06:02.620 --> 00:06:05.920
or that the perpetual lying of modern politicians is good

00:06:05.920 --> 00:06:10.080
Clinton: "I did not have sexual relations with that woman."

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:14.620
it’s that appearances and reality 
aren’t as easy to disentangle as we might think.

00:06:14.620 --> 00:06:17.360
Nothing illustrates this better than a simple fact:

00:06:17.360 --> 00:06:19.620
Frank is the epitome of a hypocrite.

00:06:19.620 --> 00:06:22.960
Frank: "Do you think I'm a hypocrite?..."

00:06:22.960 --> 00:06:24.400
"Well you should."

00:06:24.400 --> 00:06:28.580
A hypocrite is someone who acts in a way that
is inconsistent with their stated beliefs

00:06:28.580 --> 00:06:31.760
but, more specifically, puts on “false appearances”.

00:06:31.760 --> 00:06:35.400
Originally, the term comes from the
Ancient Greek word for playacting,

00:06:35.400 --> 00:06:39.020
another hint that the politics of House of Cards
is mere theater.

00:06:39.020 --> 00:06:43.420
Frank: "The road to power is paved with hypocrisy..."

00:06:43.420 --> 00:06:44.720
"...and causalities."

00:06:44.720 --> 00:06:46.200
History hates hypocrites

00:06:46.200 --> 00:06:48.660
Dante even put them in the 8th circle of hell.

00:06:48.660 --> 00:06:54.440
But rooting out hypocrites —&nbsp;the search for true motives in politics — has historically gone really really bad.

00:06:54.440 --> 00:06:59.360
The hunt for the Frank Underwoods of the world is impossible behind of the aesthetic nature of politics.

00:06:59.360 --> 00:07:04.580
As Arendt says, “The search for motives, the demand that everybody displays [their] innermost motivation...

00:07:04.580 --> 00:07:10.320
transforms all actors into hypocrites and
'hypocrisy' begins to poison all human relations.”

00:07:10.320 --> 00:07:15.660
The solution, for Arendt, is to realize that we have no ability to distinguish between “being and appearance.”

00:07:15.660 --> 00:07:20.440
After all, does Frank even know his 
true, unwavering convictions behind every action?

00:07:20.440 --> 00:07:24.060
Sure, he makes a point that power is his end game, but why?

00:07:24.060 --> 00:07:28.260
Arendt’s argument is that when you try to strip away
the façade of appearances we all adorn

00:07:28.260 --> 00:07:32.120
you don’t reveal the real person inside of us, but just more mystery.

00:07:32.120 --> 00:07:33.520
And then there’s Claire.

00:07:33.520 --> 00:07:38.360
Claire’s whole portrayal in the show suggests that, unlike Frank, there is something behind the mask,

00:07:38.360 --> 00:07:41.960
something that she must inhibit in order to continue her quest for power.

00:07:41.960 --> 00:07:46.900
When Claire sees the news that Zoe Barnes has died, she immediately turns to applying her makeup,

00:07:46.900 --> 00:07:51.080
as if aesthetics suppresses her true feelings of disgust about Frank's deeds.

00:07:51.080 --> 00:07:54.380
And while we may try in vain 
to hunt down an authentic Frank,

00:07:54.380 --> 00:07:59.800
Claire's love of art seems to indicate a yearning for authentic self expression —&nbsp;to stop performing.

00:07:59.800 --> 00:08:03.840
Claire seeks authenticity in aesthetics, like photography and origami.

00:08:03.840 --> 00:08:06.260
Even her relationship with the photographer
suggests that

00:08:06.260 --> 00:08:09.400
she's seduced by his genuine bohemian lifestyle.

00:08:09.400 --> 00:08:11.500
Part 2: Leaving a Legacy

00:08:11.500 --> 00:08:15.840
Unlike some TV characters, what drives Frank Underwood is amazingly simple:

00:08:15.840 --> 00:08:17.100
He wants power

00:08:17.100 --> 00:08:19.180
and will remind us every chance he gets.

00:08:19.180 --> 00:08:20.840
Frank: "Power is more important than money."

00:08:20.840 --> 00:08:22.960
"I'm a powerful friend to have right now."

00:08:22.960 --> 00:08:24.800
"The most you'll ever make of yourself is"

00:08:24.800 --> 00:08:27.120
"blowing men like me."

00:08:27.120 --> 00:08:29.100
"Men with REAL power."

00:08:29.100 --> 00:08:33.080
But power is the foundation of Frank’s larger dream —&nbsp;to build a legacy.

00:08:33.080 --> 00:08:38.860
We could even liken Frank’s quest for power as part of a larger quest to build a work of art in the form of his legacy.

00:08:38.860 --> 00:08:43.500
Consider the Buddhist monks sand painting in Season 3, also known as sand mandala,

00:08:43.500 --> 00:08:48.320
the practice of painstakingly creating elaborate designs and then destroying them.

00:08:48.320 --> 00:08:52.120
Frank is deeply annoyed by the Buddhist monks, and it’s no minor detail.

00:08:52.120 --> 00:08:54.000
"They'll be here for a month."

00:08:54.000 --> 00:08:54.960
Frank: "A month?"

00:08:54.960 --> 00:08:59.540
Sand mandalas stand in stark contrast to most of the Western artistic tradition,

00:08:59.540 --> 00:09:02.720
which routinely seeks to create art 
to endure through the ages

00:09:02.720 --> 00:09:04.820
with bonus point if it’s gigantic.

00:09:04.820 --> 00:09:08.100
Sand Mandalas represent the fleeting nature
of our own existence,

00:09:08.100 --> 00:09:13.180
so should we be surprised that Frank, the man who wants to achieve immortality through his quest for power

00:09:13.180 --> 00:09:13.920
hates it?

00:09:13.920 --> 00:09:14.740
Frank: "Money..."

00:09:14.740 --> 00:09:18.800
"is the McMansion in Sarasota
that starts falling apart after ten years."

00:09:18.800 --> 00:09:20.020
"Power..."

00:09:20.020 --> 00:09:24.680
"is the old stone building that stands for centuries."

00:09:24.680 --> 00:09:26.940
"I cannot respect someone who can't see the difference."

00:09:26.940 --> 00:09:30.340
Frank’s civil war diorama is an
interesting counterpoint to this —

00:09:30.340 --> 00:09:37.240
it’s a way to memorialize Frank’s ancestors, before he destroys it and he realizes it, like his presidency, is fleeting.

00:09:37.240 --> 00:09:39.720
There’s an important insight about
Claire here worth noting.

00:09:39.720 --> 00:09:42.660
She doesn’t detest the sand mandala like Frank does

00:09:42.660 --> 00:09:46.060
and its destruction seems to foreshadow
her departure from Frank.

00:09:46.060 --> 00:09:50.420
Like the mandala, Claire acknowledges that nothing, not even her marriage, is permanent.

00:09:50.420 --> 00:09:54.560
Just as the monks destroyed the thing they worked on with painstaking detail,

00:09:54.560 --> 00:10:00.420
so too does Claire leave her political life behind despite the fact she's toiled away on it for so long.

00:10:00.420 --> 00:10:03.520
Claire also struggles with legacy, but in a different way.

00:10:03.520 --> 00:10:06.420
While she values and supports
Frank’s political ambitions,

00:10:06.420 --> 00:10:12.440
she’s also discarded her own, at times throwing the CWI under the bus or shunting her desire to have kids.

00:10:12.440 --> 00:10:14.720
When Claire hands a homeless person money,

00:10:14.720 --> 00:10:18.220
itself a symbol of the power and status
that her and Frank have achieved,

00:10:18.220 --> 00:10:21.120
he rejects it and instead turns the bill into origami.

00:10:21.120 --> 00:10:24.200
Claire is essentially being confronted
by the choice she’s made:

00:10:24.200 --> 00:10:26.140
power and wealth over creation.

00:10:26.140 --> 00:10:31.420
She takes up the hobby as if a symbol of her desire desire to create a legacy through family rather than power.

00:10:31.420 --> 00:10:34.700
We could use this as an opportunity to explore
the title of the show.

00:10:34.700 --> 00:10:38.420
Is Frank building a lasting legacy or simply a house of cards

00:10:38.420 --> 00:10:41.840
whose very existence is on the verge of collapse
at any minute.

00:10:41.840 --> 00:10:43.360
Frank: "I will win."

00:10:43.360 --> 00:10:45.740
"And I will leave a legacy."

00:10:45.740 --> 00:10:48.280
Frank is essentially striving for immortality

00:10:48.280 --> 00:10:51.600
a not unfamiliar concept in the politics
of Ancient Greece

00:10:51.600 --> 00:10:52.840
Frank: "When they bury me"

00:10:52.840 --> 00:10:55.220
"it won't be in my backyard."

00:10:55.220 --> 00:10:58.300
"And when they come to pay their respects"

00:10:58.300 --> 00:11:00.240
"They'll have to wait in line."

00:11:00.240 --> 00:11:04.420
The point is to struggle to make a name for yourself through your great deeds and words.

00:11:04.420 --> 00:11:07.180
That this idea has become so alien to our society

00:11:07.180 --> 00:11:10.920
that it can only be distorted through vainglorious
characters like Frank Underwood

00:11:10.920 --> 00:11:14.180
is symptomatic of the loss of 
genuine politics of our time.

00:11:14.180 --> 00:11:16.500
Part 3: Politics as spectacle

00:11:16.500 --> 00:11:19.600
It might seem strange that a show
about America’s political system

00:11:19.600 --> 00:11:22.100
is suspiciously lacking its central tenet:

00:11:22.100 --> 00:11:22.860
Democracy

00:11:22.860 --> 00:11:27.320
In House of Cards, our political system is essentially reduced to backroom dealings and media.

00:11:27.320 --> 00:11:29.600
Frank: "Democracy is so overrated."

00:11:29.600 --> 00:11:34.180
Frank even likens Congress to a game of chess where one should keep their pieces concealed.

00:11:34.180 --> 00:11:37.640
Public opinion is important, but only as a variable to be manipulated:

00:11:37.640 --> 00:11:39.460
What color should Claire’s hair be?

00:11:39.460 --> 00:11:40.280
Claire: "Whadda ya think."

00:11:40.280 --> 00:11:41.180
Frank: I don't like it.

00:11:41.180 --> 00:11:44.480
What do people think of Peter Russo’s 
phoenix-out-of-ashes story?

00:11:44.480 --> 00:11:47.700
At other times, public opinion is something that can
be simply turned off.

00:11:47.700 --> 00:11:52.280
Even the media, which our Constitution specifically
protects as integral to our democracy,

00:11:52.280 --> 00:11:54.660
is just part of a larger political game.

00:11:54.660 --> 00:11:58.800
Zoe Barnes is fed information that’s beneficial to Frank’s political machinations

00:11:58.800 --> 00:12:01.820
Tom Yates is hired to write
what is essentially propaganda

00:12:01.820 --> 00:12:02.780
the list goes on.

00:12:02.780 --> 00:12:06.640
There are, frankly, too many thinkers who
tackle this problem to cover here.

00:12:06.640 --> 00:12:11.340
German philosopher Carl Schmitt argued that things like debate and openness have become

00:12:11.340 --> 00:12:14.620
mere spectacle for the backroom dealings of party leaders

00:12:14.620 --> 00:12:16.340
Frank: "You're finally one of us."

00:12:16.340 --> 00:12:19.260
"The men in their smoky backrooms."

00:12:19.260 --> 00:12:21.460
But one philosopher takes this a step further.

00:12:21.460 --> 00:12:24.640
While lots of people argue that deception masks an inner reality,

00:12:24.640 --> 00:12:28.980
French philosopher Jean Baudrillard argues that there is no truth behind the lies.

00:12:28.980 --> 00:12:34.940
For Baudrillard, to have a lie concealing a truth is child’s play compared to our modern media nightmare.

00:12:34.940 --> 00:12:38.240
Lies told by politicians and media take on lives of their own,

00:12:38.240 --> 00:12:41.700
until information circulates like
commodities on the stock market.

00:12:41.700 --> 00:12:44.240
Frank and Zoe literally barter for information.

00:12:44.240 --> 00:12:46.900
Before getting canned, her boss at the
newspaper claims:

00:12:46.900 --> 00:12:48.700
"Zoe Barnes..."

00:12:48.700 --> 00:12:50.160
"Twitter..."

00:12:50.160 --> 00:12:51.180
"Blogs..."

00:12:51.180 --> 00:12:52.940
"Enriched media..."

00:12:52.940 --> 00:12:54.020
"They're all surface."

00:12:54.020 --> 00:12:54.880
"They're fads."

00:12:54.880 --> 00:12:59.040
As if to prove the integrity and depth are relegated
to the dustbin of history.

00:12:59.040 --> 00:13:04.480
Slugline, the new standard for media, is more concerned about seeming edgy and bleeding edge

00:13:04.480 --> 00:13:08.640
so much so, that Zoe’s boss informs her
that her work won’t even be checked.

00:13:08.640 --> 00:13:10.180
Zoe: "Oh, I thought you might wanna take a look."

00:13:10.180 --> 00:13:13.300
Carly: "The goal here is for everyone to post things faster than I have a chance read them."

00:13:13.300 --> 00:13:15.760
"If you're satisfied with the article, you just put it up."

00:13:15.760 --> 00:13:20.720
Baudrillard says: "Where we think that information produces meaning, the opposite occurs.

00:13:20.720 --> 00:13:23.600
Information devours it's own content."

00:13:23.600 --> 00:13:24.300
Zoe: "Is that true?"

00:13:24.300 --> 00:13:25.960
Frank: "It will be after you write it."

00:13:25.960 --> 00:13:30.700
More than just existing for its own sake, this circuit of information is seductive.

00:13:30.700 --> 00:13:37.120
Frank Underwood is seductive, not only to his former classmate, Tom Yeats, and Meecham, but to us the viewer.

00:13:37.120 --> 00:13:41.460
Frank: "Great man once said, that everything in life is about sex..."

00:13:41.460 --> 00:13:43.460
"except sex."

00:13:43.460 --> 00:13:45.740
"Sex is about power."

00:13:45.740 --> 00:13:50.520
Frank knocking his ring at the end of Season 2 leaves us confounded with a feeling of awe.

00:13:50.520 --> 00:13:54.980
If we, too, are seduced by Frank Underwood, what does that say about our political system?

00:13:54.980 --> 00:13:56.720
Do we enjoy the performance?

00:13:56.720 --> 00:14:00.520
Are we doomed to fall in love with the
real Frank Underwoods of the world?

00:14:00.520 --> 00:14:01.380
Conclusion

00:14:01.380 --> 00:14:05.720
If House of Cards is so good, it’s because it has so many incredible layers to it.

00:14:05.720 --> 00:14:09.800
It’s ultimately a show about the politics of appearance, whether through art or theater.

00:14:09.800 --> 00:14:12.200
Whether or not that’s a bad thing is up for debate

00:14:12.200 --> 00:14:13.460
but so far it's...

00:14:13.460 --> 00:14:14.580
looking pretty bad.

